{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>but there is none who interprets it.</b> Meaning, he did have interpreters, only the dream would not prove correct according to the interpretations of the interpreters. And this is what the verse says — <b>but there is none who interprets it</b> — and the intention in this is that he should give an interpretation that will prove correct with the dream." "<b>And I have heard, etc.</b> — one needs to understand his saying <b>saying.</b> Further, his saying <b>you hear</b> [in ״תִּשְׁמַע״] — it is obvious that he would not interpret before hearing, and he should have said: ״I heard about you that you interpret dreams.״" "<b>However,</b> the intention is that Pharaoh contrived to obligate Joseph to interpret the dream without giving him any pretext. He came with the claim, saying that Joseph himself — his own mouth testified that he is an interpreter of dreams, and from now on he cannot exempt himself: either he will interpret Pharaoh's dream, or he will stand accountable for having deceived people that he is an interpreter when there is nothing sound in his mouth. And this is what he said — <b>And I have heard about you, saying</b> — meaning, you yourself say, ״you hear a dream to interpret it,״ as indeed was truly the case when he later said to the chief cupbearer and the chief baker (Genesis 40:7): ״Tell me, please,״ etc. And presumably the chief cupbearer reported these matters exactly as they were to Pharaoh; or Pharaoh tested that his ministers would not tell the dreams of a Hebrew lad, a slave, unless he himself asked to tell them. And from now on he is obligated to interpret Pharaoh's dream, to vindicate his words."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:30.970125197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}